WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.540
Where does a vanished trait go when two lines cross?

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.600
Cross round peas with wrinkled ones, and every hybrid is round.

3
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.820
Genes stay hidden, so biologists count offspring from planned crosses.

4
00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.630
Controlled crosses start with pure lines.

5
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.450
Garden pea flowers enclose their reproductive organs inside petals, so they normally

6
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:29.530
self-pollinate. Left alone, a line stays pure.

7
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.870
Opened by hand, it can be crossed without stray pollen.

8
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:39.033
Next, let the round hybrids self-pollinate, and count every seed.

9
00:00:40.493 --> 00:00:44.093
Gregor Mendel counted 7,324 seeds.

10
00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:50.913
5,474 were round, and 1,850 were wrinkled.

11
00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:56.373
Round divided by wrinkled is 2.96, nearly 3:1.

12
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.827
Each plant carries two copies of each gene; the versions are called alleles.

13
00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:06.327
A hybrid with two different alleles is a heterozygote.

14
00:01:07.727 --> 00:01:11.147
Segregation deals one allele per gamete like a fair coin toss.

15
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:15.647
Two heterozygotes fill four equal Punnett square boxes.

16
00:01:17.007 --> 00:01:21.547
With complete dominance, one round copy shows: 3 round to 1 wrinkled.

17
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:27.550
Planned crosses test inheritance by comparing offspring counts to expected

18
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:33.170
probabilities. Add an unlinked second trait, seed colour, with its own coin.

19
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:39.150
Each hybrid makes four gamete types at 1/4 each, dealing sixteen boxes.

20
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.930
Dominance groups them into a 9 to 3 to 3 to 1 ratio.

21
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:50.293
Mendel counted 556 hybrid seeds across those four classes.

22
00:01:51.613 --> 00:01:55.913
He got 315, 108, 101, and 32.

23
00:01:57.373 --> 00:02:03.173
Nine sixteenths of 556 predicts 312.75 round yellow.

24
00:02:04.493 --> 00:02:05.573
None matched exactly.

25
00:02:06.873 --> 00:02:08.353
Does that mismatch break the rule?

26
00:02:14.333 --> 00:02:20.613
Not by itself. A cross predicts probabilities, which make a spread of counts, not

27
00:02:20.613 --> 00:02:26.733
exact matches. A chi-square score tests whether counts sit inside that usual spread.

28
00:02:28.013 --> 00:02:32.993
When a fixed ratio holds, chi-square averages classes minus one, or 3 here.

29
00:02:33.873 --> 00:02:36.713
Higher scores give reason to doubt, never proof.

30
00:02:37.817 --> 00:02:40.457
Hidden assumptions can still mislead a cross.

31
00:02:41.957 --> 00:02:46.497
Genes close together on one chromosome travel together, breaking independent

32
00:02:46.497 --> 00:02:53.117
assortment. Standard ratios also assume equal survival, equal transmission, and

33
00:02:53.117 --> 00:02:58.497
accurate scoring. Swapping which parent carries a trait checks whether parental

34
00:02:58.497 --> 00:02:59.497
origin matters.

35
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:05.610
Today, Mendel's pea counts are read as segregation and independent assortment.

36
00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:11.570
In the first years after, Lucien Cuénot's mouse crosses seemed to break Mendel's

37
00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.950
rules. They did not.

38
00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:20.810
Two yellow alleles kill the embryo, dropping one box, so every live yellow mouse is

39
00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:21.810
a heterozygote.

40
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:28.200
In Morgan's laboratory at Columbia University, a white-eyed Drosophila male was

41
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.580
crossed to red-eyed females.

42
00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.720
Red-eyed offspring of both sexes showed white is recessive.

43
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:39.380
He published the work in Science as sex limited inheritance.

44
00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.100
Fitting or breaking expectations, counts expose how traits are inherited.
